<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu al-Makarim Hibat Allah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu al-Makarim Hibat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> was the chief of an Arab tribal group of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a> settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s southern frontier around Aswan. In 1006 the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim gave him the title Kanz al-Dawla for capturing the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-rakwa">Abu Rakwa</a>, and his successors took their name, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kanz">Banu Kanz</a>, from it. They ruled the Aswan frontier and its Nubian marches for centuries.</p></div>
